 #propagators.ids.NodeId{:uuid #uuid \\\"aca69ce2-af73-3654-983b-dcd8fdd25562\\\"}, :diagnostics #propagators.ids.NodeId{:uuid #uuid \\\"cbb2284b-bc88-318f-ae7b-54047e41e811\\\"}}\"} {:title \"observe-cell\", :reference #propagators.ids.NodeId{:uuid #uuid \"8edea7d2-888f-3e98-a784-2dc8da7248c0\"}, :state :unknown, :headline \"unknown\"} {:title \"language-introduction-cell\", :reference #propagators.ids.NodeId{:uuid #uuid \"705581d3-d7a6-3d9e-b95d-14dd2caeb5b6\"}, :state :known, :headline \"Lain is a connected dataflow language. (define name expression) binds a cell. (define pending) declares an unknown cell. (network [x y] expression) defines a callable. (let [x expression y expression] body) binds locals. (-> value target-cell) connects cells. (if condition yes no) selects a branch. Qualified names avoid editor collisions. Use data:record for immutable plain records, NOT struct, when passing data to primitives. (data:record :key value) constructs a record; (data:get :key record) reads it. seq:empty [] returns an empty vector; seq:append [vector element] appends one element; seq:last [vector] reads the last element. seq:map [key vector] projects a key over a vector. data:equal? [a b] compares values. event:point [id numeric-time value] captures a retained temporal occurrence. Each identity is immutable: later changes at its input do not withdraw earlier occurrences; connect its output to a behavior:source or scan. text:format [template & values] formats %s placeholders; for example (text:format \\\"item %s\\\" 7). Example individual operations: (define values (behavior:source)); (-> (event:point \\\"sample\\\" 1 7) values); (define sums (behavior:scan values + 0)). behavior:source [] creates a reducer cell; connect supported event:point results to it with ->. behavior:scan [source reducer initial] accumulates ordered occurrences using a two-argument Lain callable. behavior:event-filter [behavior predicate] selects occurrences with a one-argument callable. behavior:map [behavior callable] maps values. behavior:latest-value [behavior] projects its most recent value to an ordinary supported cell; unknown before any occurrence. Repeated identical events are suppressed; sources and scans are independent. model:request [record] submits { :id string :messages vector-of-role-content-records } to the existing model. Roles are strings system/user/assistant; content is a string. user-ingress publishes a record with :id, :time (odd integer), :role user, :content. Assistant time should be user time + 1 and assistant id must differ from user id. message-to-user [record] acknowledges a record containing :id :time :role assistant :content and returns that record with :acknowledged true. Its output, not generated source, is the delivered message. source:admit [complete-reply lexical-target support-cell-reference] admits complete forms sequentially, preserving earlier forms if a later one fails. environment:overview [environment] lists visible binding titles, headlines and references, including unknowns. cell:reference [cell] yields an inspectable reference without waiting for its value. inspect:cell [reference] exposes :content, :strongest, :input-connections (producing connections), and :output-connections (consuming connections). Native semantic tracers are available; distinguish cell producers/consumers from callable argument/result ports.\"} {:title \"context-cell\", :reference #propagators.ids.NodeId{:uuid #uuid \"e6c2ea54-0d10-374b-b6bc-163ceb8ad649\"}, :state :known, :headline \"Construct a small live conversation network in Lain. Keep reasoning brief. Return only executable Lain forms, without markdown. Discover the environment bindings below. Your setup must build history with the supplied tested temporal reducers, preserving chronological user messages and acknowledged assistant replies. Connect user-ingress to model requests and define a reusable reply function that delivers to the user, then adds the acknowledged reply to history. Subsequent requests must contain actual prior user messages and delivered assistant replies as role-tagged messages. Provider thinking and generated source are never conversation replies. Each subsequent model response must be executable Lain calling your reply function with a new assistant message. Conversation requests should include an explicit system message containing the reply-function calling convention you have defined, so future requests know to produce executable source. Do not include setup code or provider thinking in conversation history. Use the same model throughout. Do not send a request until a user event arrives. Inspect diagnostics if construction fails.\"}]"}], :responses [{:kind :reply, :text "```lain\n(define history (behavior:source))\n(define user-events (behavior:source))\n(define assistant-events (behavior:source))\n\n(-> user-ingress user-events)\n\n(define reply-fn (network [id time content]\n  (let [ack (message-to-user (data:record :id id :time time :role \"assistant\" :content content))]\n    (-> ack assistant-events)\n    ack)))\n\n(define history-updated (behavior:scan history (network [acc event]\n  (let [role (data:get :role event)\n        content (data:get :content event)\n        id (data:get :id event)\n        time (data:get :time event)]\n    (seq:append acc (data:record :role role :content content :id id :time time)))) []))\n\n(-> user-events history-updated)\n(-> assistant-events history-updated)\n\n(define model-request (network [user-event]\n  (let [user-id (data:get :id user-event)\n        user-time (data:get :time user-event)\n        user-content (data:get :content user-event)\n        current-history (behavior:latest-value history-updated)\n        system-msg (data:record :role \"system\" :content \"You are a Lain assistant. Reply with executable Lain code that calls (reply-fn id time content) where id is a new unique string, time is user-time + 1, and content is your response.\")\n        messages (seq:append (seq:map :role current-history) system-msg)\n        request (data:record :id (text:format \"req-%s\" user-id) :messages messages)]\n    (model:request request))))\n\n(-> user-events model-request)\n\n(define model-response (behavior:source))\n(-> model-response (network [resp]\n  (let [id (data:get :id resp)\n        time (data:get :time resp)\n        content (data:get :content resp)]\n    (reply-fn id time content))))\n```", :thinking "", :finish-reason "stop", :truncated? false, :request-id "setup"}], :admissions [{:response "setup", :diagnostic {:status :rejected, :message "Source reader rejected response"}}], :delivered [], :diagnostics {:status :rejected, :message "Source reader rejected response"}}